WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:06.129
There's no denying it: Director Andy Muschietti's
2017 adaptation of Stephen King's It was fifty

00:00:06.129 --> 00:00:07.169
shades of freaky.

00:00:07.169 --> 00:00:12.360
Well, prepare for things to get scarier, bloodier,
and way more traumatizing in the film's upcoming

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.059
sequel, It: Chapter Two.

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:18.500
It's just been confirmed that two extremely
controversial aspects of King's 1986 novel

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.340
have made it into the film.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.789
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:26.539
Speaking to The Hollywood Reporter, screenwriter
Gary Dauberman revealed that It: Chapter Two

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:30.330
will include one of the most haunting scenes
in the book: - A horrific hate crime against

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:35.000
a young gay man named Adrian Mellon, who will
be portrayed by actor, writer, and director

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.320
Xavier Dolan.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.040
In the book, Mellon becomes the victim of
a savage beating at the hands of three homophobic

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.329
teenagers, who throw him over a bridge.

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:45.699
Then, he's subsequently murdered by Pennywise
the Dancing Clown.

00:00:45.699 --> 00:00:50.480
This subplot was conspicuously absent from
the 1990 television adaptation of It, but

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.289
the creative team behind the upcoming film
thought it was important to include Mellon's

00:00:54.289 --> 00:00:55.289
story.

00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:56.630
As Dauberman explained to The Hollywood Reporter:

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:59.909
"It is an iconic scene in the book and one
we wanted to include in the movie.

00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:04.269
[...] It is the influence of Pennywise even
while he is hibernating, and it's pure evil

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:05.830
what happens to Adrian.

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:09.010
These bullies working through Pennywise was
important for us to show."

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:13.400
Another contentious aspect of King's novel
is the plight of adult Beverly Marsh, portrayed

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.680
by Jessica Chastain.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.790
The second part of King's novel jumps ahead
27 years, and we learn that Marsh has become

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.160
a domestic abuse victim at the hands of her
husband Tom Rogan.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.280
We now know this storyline is included in
It: Chapter Two, and the film closely mirrors

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.640
the events of the book.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.630
As Dauberman explained, the whole production
wanted to remain true to the original novel.

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:35.210
It's safe to say the It sequel will outdo
the first installment on every front.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.470
Dauberman promises that

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:41.100
"[The film is] definitely bloodier than the
first and it is just as scary, if not scarier."

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.750
Muschietti clearly agrees.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:46.500
Over a year ago, he warned fans that they
should prepare to be rocked to the core, claiming

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.500
that,

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:51.700
"It's going to be scarier and more intense,
so bring your adult diapers to the movie theater."

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.890
Two more tidbits are sure to pique the interest
of any gorehound.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:59.060
The film evidently features a scene that's
doused in a shocking amount of stage blood…

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.580
and Pennywise will reportedly be far more
savage this time around.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.860
Chastain described the bloody sequence in
an interview with Jimmy Fallon:

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:10.610
“There's a scene that someone said on set
that it's the most blood that's ever been

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:14.010
in a horror film — in a scene."

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:18.250
Meanwhile, Pennywise actor Bill Skarsgård
told Entertainment Weekly that the creature

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:22.730
has only become stronger in the 27 years it
spent lying dormant in the Derry sewers.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.690
And whereas Pennywise was once terrorizing
the children for sport, the ghastly creature

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.480
is now motivated by revenge.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.480
As Skarsgård explains:

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.980
"It fuels hatred and anger towards the kids,
who will be adults in this one, so I think

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.030
there might be an even more vicious Pennywise.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.280
He's really going after it."

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.130
It sounds like It: Chapter Two is going to
be truly terrifying, and the inclusion of

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:45.510
those two unsettling subplots from the book
will just make the film even more upsetting.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.720
See how everything plays out when It: Chapter
Two floats into theaters on September 6th.

